#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/getting_started/tutorial-4-setting-parameter-values.ipynb:1081
msgid "Input parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/getting_started/tutorial-4-setting-parameter-values.ipynb:1083
msgid ""
"If the value of a parameter is expected to change often (e.g. running a "
"parameter sweep) is is more convenient to set a parameter as an \"input "
"parameter\". This is a placeholder that can be filled in with a numerical "
"value when the model is solved."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/getting_started/tutorial-4-setting-parameter-values.ipynb:1085
msgid ""
"To set a parameter as an input parameter, we can set its value to the string "
"``[input]`` in the parameter values dictionary. For example, we can set the "
"``Current function [A]`` to be an input parameter and then run a parameter "
"sweep over different current values like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/getting_started/tutorial-4-setting-parameter-values.ipynb:-1
msgid "[12]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/getting_started/tutorial-4-setting-parameter-values.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n"
"\n"
"parameter_values[\"Current function [A]\"] = \"[input]\"\n"
"sim = pybamm.Simulation(model, parameter_values=parameter_values)\n"
"solns = []\n"
"for c in [0.1, 0.2, 0.3]:\n"
"    soln = sim.solve([0, 3600], inputs={\"Current function [A]\": c})\n"
"    plt.plot(soln[\"Time [s]\"].entries, soln[\"Voltage [V]\"].entries, "
"label=f\"{c} A\")\n"
"    solns.append(soln[\"Terminal voltage [V]\"].entries)\n"
"plt.xlabel(\"Time [s]\")\n"
"plt.ylabel(\"Terminal voltage [V]\")\n"
"plt.legend()\n"
"plt.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/getting_started/tutorial-4-setting-parameter-values.ipynb:1127
msgid "Define a new parameter set"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/getting_started/tutorial-4-setting-parameter-values.ipynb:1129
msgid ""
"We can also define a new parameter set from scratch, which is useful if "
"there is a new battery or chemistry on which we need to run simulations "
"repeatedly. To do so, we can initialise a ``ParameterValues`` object and "
"pass as an argument a dictionary of parameter values:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/getting_started/tutorial-4-setting-parameter-values.ipynb:-1
msgid "[13]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/getting_started/tutorial-4-setting-parameter-values.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"def cube(t):\n"
"    return t**3\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"parameter_values = pybamm.ParameterValues(\n"
"    {\n"
"        \"Negative electrode thickness [m]\": 1e-4,\n"
"        \"Positive electrode thickness [m]\": 1.2e-4,\n"
"        \"Current function [A]\": cube,\n"
"    }\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/getting_started/tutorial-4-setting-parameter-values.ipynb:1160
msgid ""
"Note how, when we pass a function as a parameter, we pass the object without "
"calling it, i.e. we pass ``cube`` rather than ``cube(t)``. This new "
"``parameter_values`` variable could now be passed to a simulation, but note "
"that it is incomplete as it does not include all the parameters that the "
"model needs to run (see the parameters needed by calling ``model."
"print_parameter_info()``, as done above)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/getting_started/tutorial-4-setting-parameter-values.ipynb:1162
msgid ""
"It is often convenient to define the parameter set in a separate file, and "
"then call the parameters into your notebook or script. You can find some "
"examples on how to do so in `PyBaMM's parameter library <https://github.com/"
"pybamm-team/PyBaMM/tree/main/packages/pybamm/src/pybamm/input/parameters/"
"lithium_ion>`__. You can copy one of the parameter sets available into a new "
"file and modify it accordingly for the new parameter set. Then, whenever the "
"set is needed, one can import the ``get_parameter_values`` method from the "
"corresponding file and call it to obtain a copy of the parameter values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/getting_started/tutorial-4-setting-parameter-values.ipynb:1174
msgid ""
"In this notebook we have seen how we can change the parameters of our model. "
"In `Tutorial 5 <./tutorial-5-run-experiments.ipynb>`__ we show how can we "
"define and run experiments."
msgstr ""

